WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:04.880
Everyone knows Green Goblin from the&nbsp;
Spider-Man movies. But it's a special&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:10.240
few who know about his time in Spider-Punk's&nbsp;
Spider-Army during the "Edge of Spider-Geddon."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:12.800
Curious what we're talking about? Keep watching.

00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:17.680
Nowadays, it might seem like common&nbsp;
knowledge that Norman Osborn is the Green&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:21.760
Goblin, but the Goblin's identity was&nbsp;
once actually a mystery to readers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:27.920
From the character's first appearance in Amazing&nbsp;
Spider-Man #14, it would be two years, 25 issues,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.920 --> 00:00:32.560
and one major artist change before the&nbsp;
Green Goblin was unmasked. That artist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.560 --> 00:00:37.680
change even helped feed an urban legend about&nbsp;
the Goblin — one that has survived for years.

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:43.760
Amazing Spider-Man #38 was Spidey co-creator&nbsp;
Steve Ditko's final issue as the comic's artist.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:49.440
John Romita Sr. replaced Ditko with Amazing&nbsp;
Spider-Man #39, which happens to be the issue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:55.280
in which Goblin's identity is revealed. Goblin&nbsp;
being unmasked as soon as Ditko left the title&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.280 --> 00:01:00.720
led many fans to believe that Ditko was against&nbsp;
Osborn being Green Goblin, and that this was why&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:05.920
he left. Spider-Man's other co-creator has&nbsp;
seemed to confirm half of this fan theory.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.920 --> 00:01:11.680
In a 2011 issue of the magazine Alter Ego, Stan&nbsp;
Lee said Ditko wanted Goblin to be "just some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.680 --> 00:01:16.560
character that we had never seen before," but&nbsp;
Lee insisted that would be cheating readers.

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:21.040
However, by the time Ditko left Amazing&nbsp;
Spider-Man, he and Lee were embroiled in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.040 --> 00:01:25.280
so many financial conflicts that Ditko's&nbsp;
departure from the title was inevitable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:30.880
regardless of any creative differences. That&nbsp;
said, in a 2009 essay, Ditko not only denied&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.880 --> 00:01:35.920
Lee's claims that he was against Osborn being&nbsp;
the Goblin, but says he always knew Osborn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:40.800
would be the Goblin, and that he made plenty of&nbsp;
creative choices to facilitate that future reveal.

00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:46.400
Norman Osborn is hardly the only character&nbsp;
to bear the name Green Goblin. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:50.320
while he's the original, most memorable,&nbsp;
and most enduring version of the character,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:53.680
he's only one of five different&nbsp;
characters to have used the name.

00:01:53.680 --> 00:01:59.520
Just as Norman's son Harry becomes New Goblin in&nbsp;
2007's Spider-Man 3, Harry also assumes the name&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:06.000
of Green Goblin in 1974's Amazing Spider-Man #136,&nbsp;
after discovering Peter Parker was Spider-Man.

00:02:06.880 --> 00:02:10.480
"Look at the little Goblin Jr. Gonna cry?"

00:02:14.480 --> 00:02:18.960
The third Goblin is Dr. Bart Hamilton,&nbsp;
a psychiatrist who treats Harry Osborn.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.960 --> 00:02:23.760
Hamilton uses hypnosis to learn Green Goblin's&nbsp;
secrets, including the location of his hideout and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:28.560
equipment. Visiting the hideout, Hamilton gives&nbsp;
in to temptation and becomes the next Goblin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:32.560
beginning in 1978's Amazing Spider-Man #176.

00:02:32.560 --> 00:02:36.480
Green Goblin #4 tries his hand&nbsp;
at the hero business. Phil Urich,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:40.160
son of Daily Bugle reporter Ben Urich,&nbsp;
recovers the Goblin equipment after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:44.000
Harry Osborn's death and even&nbsp;
enjoys a short-lived solo title.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:50.000
Urich retires after his equipment is damaged&nbsp;
beyond repair during the 1996 Onslaught event.

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:54.160
After returning from the dead for a few&nbsp;
decades, Norman Osborn orders the creation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.160 --> 00:02:59.840
of a genetically engineered minion, designed to&nbsp;
be the new Green Goblin, in 2000's Peter Parker:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.840 --> 00:03:04.480
Spider-Man #18. He dresses as the Goblin&nbsp;
and acts like the Goblin — all to help&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.480 --> 00:03:08.400
Osborn convince the world he was never&nbsp;
the Green Goblin in the first place.

00:03:09.200 --> 00:03:13.840
Eventually, Green Goblin became more&nbsp;
than just Spider-Man's enemy. How? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.840 --> 00:03:19.040
as it happened, he was helped out by a bunch&nbsp;
of green skinned guys with pointy ears.

00:03:19.040 --> 00:03:23.920
During the 2006 Civil War event, Norman&nbsp;
Osborn is appointed director of a new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.920 --> 00:03:28.880
version of the Thunderbolts, a group tasked&nbsp;
with capturing unregistered superheroes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.880 --> 00:03:32.960
Osborn's Thunderbolts include villains who&nbsp;
are little nastier than the team is used to,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.960 --> 00:03:36.960
including the kill-crazy villain&nbsp;
Bullseye and the deadly symbiote Venom.

00:03:36.960 --> 00:03:41.440
Osborn's leadership role in the Thunderbolts&nbsp;
puts him in a position to be in the right place&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.440 --> 00:03:46.560
at the right time when Secret Invasion comes&nbsp;
along. Focused on the shape-shifting Skrulls,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.560 --> 00:03:51.120
Secret Invasion has most of Marvel's heroes&nbsp;
uniting against this new alien threat.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.120 --> 00:03:55.840
Osborn later succeeds in gaining the public's&nbsp;
trust by shooting and killing the Skrull queen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.840 --> 00:04:00.160
Veranke. When the dust settles, Tony Stark&nbsp;
is blamed for the Skrull infiltration&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.160 --> 00:04:04.240
and ousted as director of S.H.I.E.L.D.&nbsp;
while Osborn is made the head of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.240 --> 00:04:08.080
new military force H.A.M.M.E.R. From&nbsp;
there, his power keeps on growing.

00:04:08.720 --> 00:04:14.000
When Green Goblin gains control of H.A.M.M.E.R.,&nbsp;
the Superhero Registration Act is still in effect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:17.440
and most of the surviving former&nbsp;
Avengers are considered outlaws.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.440 --> 00:04:22.400
Osborn takes advantage of this situation&nbsp;
and forms his own team of Dark Avengers.

00:04:22.400 --> 00:04:26.720
Most of Osborn's Dark Avengers are villains&nbsp;
renamed and rebranded as the replacements&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.720 --> 00:04:31.760
for more traditional Avengers. Bullseye is&nbsp;
disguised as Hawkeye, Daken as Wolverine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.760 --> 00:04:36.720
Venom as Spider-Man, and Moonstone as&nbsp;
Ms. Marvel. Noh-Varr, a.k.a. Marvel Boy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:41.520
joins the team as a new Captain Marvel. The&nbsp;
only holdouts from pre-Osborn Avengers teams&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.520 --> 00:04:46.320
are the mentally unstable Sentry and&nbsp;
the morally questionable war god Ares.

00:04:46.320 --> 00:04:50.800
Osborn's influence is finally put&nbsp;
to bed at the conclusion of the 2010&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.800 --> 00:04:55.440
line-wide event Siege, in which Osborn&nbsp;
leads a failed invasion of Asgard — and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.440 --> 00:04:58.240
is finally arrested and imprisoned for his crimes.

00:04:58.960 --> 00:05:00.960
"It tested well with focus groups, alright?"

00:05:00.960 --> 00:05:01.920
"I am Iron Patriot!"

00:05:02.560 --> 00:05:07.760
If you've seen Iron Man 3, then you'll already&nbsp;
know the name Iron Patriot. What you may not know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.760 --> 00:05:12.560
is that, in the comics, Iron Patriot originally&nbsp;
had nothing to do with Colonel James Rhodes.

00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:18.320
Combining the design of Stark's Iron Man armor&nbsp;
with the colors of Captain America, Norman Osborn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.320 --> 00:05:24.160
creates the Iron Patriot armor so he can use it to&nbsp;
lead his Dark Avengers. The armor gives him most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.160 --> 00:05:29.040
of Iron Man's abilities, though unlike Stark's&nbsp;
armor, it isn't based on repulsor technology.

00:05:29.040 --> 00:05:34.000
Norman also creates the American Son armor for&nbsp;
his son Harry, but the younger Osborn would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:38.320
eventually discover that Norman did it just&nbsp;
so he could eventually kill Harry in order to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.320 --> 00:05:44.720
create public sympathy for himself. Norman remains&nbsp;
Iron Patriot until his plans fall apart in Siege.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.720 --> 00:05:48.800
He eventually escapes from The Raft and&nbsp;
forms a new team of Dark Avengers in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.800 --> 00:05:53.360
hopes of recapturing his old suit and getting&nbsp;
his revenge on the heroes who defeated him.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.360 --> 00:05:57.440
He fails, of course, and soon after&nbsp;
resumes his life as the Green Goblin.

00:05:58.160 --> 00:06:03.520
Norman Osborn's most sickeningly powerful&nbsp;
transformation comes at the end of 2018's Amazing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.520 --> 00:06:09.280
Spider-Man #798. Osborn is one of the first&nbsp;
people to figure out Peter Parker is Spider-Man,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.280 --> 00:06:14.080
but the events of the 2007 storyline One&nbsp;
More Day takes that knowledge away from him.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.080 --> 00:06:20.560
In Amazing Spider-Man #797, when J. Jonah Jameson&nbsp;
mentions Green Goblin's killing of Gwen Stacy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.560 --> 00:06:24.560
the words spark something in Osborn and&nbsp;
cause him to remember who Spider-Man&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.560 --> 00:06:27.920
is. With this new revelation,&nbsp;
Goblin demands Spidey quit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.920 --> 00:06:31.920
being a hero — or he'll kill all the&nbsp;
important people in Parker's life.

00:06:31.920 --> 00:06:36.880
Osborn also attacks the offices of the Daily Bugle&nbsp;
and, after an intense battle with Spider-Man,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.880 --> 00:06:41.920
winds up impaled on debris. Spidey thinks&nbsp;
Goblin's dying, but the issue ends with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.920 --> 00:06:46.960
Carnage symbiote appearing and bonding&nbsp;
with Osborn to create the Red Goblin.

00:06:46.960 --> 00:06:52.240
As the Red Goblin, Osborn is more powerful than&nbsp;
ever and doesn't even suffer the usual symbiote&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.240 --> 00:06:57.520
vulnerabilities to fire and sound. Red Goblin&nbsp;
continues the hunt for Parker's loved ones, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.520 --> 00:07:02.800
Spidey is only able to prevent the killing of Aunt&nbsp;
May and MJ with the help of Venom, Anti-Venom, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.800 --> 00:07:08.720
the Superior Octopus. Borrowing Venom's symbiote&nbsp;
and allied with his old friend Flash Thompson,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.720 --> 00:07:13.600
Spidey is finally able to defeat the Red Goblin&nbsp;
— although Flash is killed during the battle.

00:07:14.240 --> 00:07:19.840
The 2014 Marvel Comics event Spider-Verse&nbsp;
introduced dozens of alternate reality versions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.840 --> 00:07:25.200
of Spider-Man. Then, in 2018, Spider-Geddon&nbsp;
brought in even more alternate Spideys,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.200 --> 00:07:28.000
including a Norman Osborn&nbsp;
version of the character.

00:07:28.000 --> 00:07:35.760
Edge of Spider-Geddon #4 introduces the Spider-Man&nbsp;
of Earth-44145. In that reality, Norman Osborn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.760 --> 00:07:41.120
gained spider abilities instead of Peter Parker,&nbsp;
and even grew four extra arms in the process.

00:07:41.120 --> 00:07:46.800
This version of Norman Osborn is forcefully&nbsp;
recruited into the new Spider-Army by Spider-Punk,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.800 --> 00:07:50.880
but by the end of Spider-Geddon it seems clear&nbsp;
that everyone would have been better off leaving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.880 --> 00:07:58.880
him at home. Allying himself only with Spiders-Man&nbsp;
of Earth-11580, the Osborn-Spidey abandons the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.880 --> 00:08:03.520
Spider Army and chooses to destroy the Web of&nbsp;
Life and Destiny that allows the different Spider&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.520 --> 00:08:09.040
heroes to travel between other realities. After&nbsp;
Spider-Geddon, this character acts as the final&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.040 --> 00:08:14.800
adversary for the reformed Otto Octavius&nbsp;
in the 2019 volume of Superior Spider-Man.

00:08:15.520 --> 00:08:20.400
Most recently in the comics, Norman Osborn&nbsp;
has turned to the side of the angels — though&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.400 --> 00:08:24.800
you could make the argument it wasn't by his own&nbsp;
choice. Shortly after being made the director of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.800 --> 00:08:30.880
Ravencroft Institute by Mayor Wilson Fisk, Osborn&nbsp;
finds himself in the crosshairs of a resurrected&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.880 --> 00:08:36.720
vigilante. The supernatural villain Kindred had&nbsp;
brought the Sin-Eater back to life, and this time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.720 --> 00:08:42.480
he has powers worthy of his namesake. Rather than&nbsp;
just killing his prey, the new Sin-Eater can wipe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.480 --> 00:08:49.120
away his target's sins, which is precisely what he&nbsp;
does to Norman in 2020's Amazing Spider-Man #50.

00:08:49.120 --> 00:08:54.400
While he believes Sin-Eater is going to kill him,&nbsp;
Norman instead wakes to find his evil urges have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.400 --> 00:08:59.040
disappeared, replaced only with remorse&nbsp;
for everything he's done. Understandably,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.040 --> 00:09:02.800
it takes Spider-Man a while to accept&nbsp;
that Osborn's changes are genuine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.800 --> 00:09:08.080
and the wall-crawler beats the heck out&nbsp;
of him in 2021's Amazing Spider-Man #57.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.080 --> 00:09:11.200
Spidey fortunately stops&nbsp;
himself before he goes too far.

00:09:11.200 --> 00:09:15.600
For the time being, Osborn seems to be on the&nbsp;
right side of things. In fact, he does what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.600 --> 00:09:20.880
little he can to help Spider-Man during the 2021&nbsp;
Sinister War event, but considering who he is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.880 --> 00:09:24.160
it's probably only a matter of time&nbsp;
before the Green Goblin flies again.

00:09:24.960 --> 00:09:30.080
Nearly two decades after 2002's Spider-Man&nbsp;
helped usher in the dominance of super-heroes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.080 --> 00:09:33.440
on the big screen, Willem Dafoe said&nbsp;
some pretty wonderful things to say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.440 --> 00:09:38.160
about his experience playing the Green&nbsp;
Goblin. Speaking to GQ in February 2019,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.160 --> 00:09:42.240
Dafoe talked about how the Osborn/Green&nbsp;
Goblin duality appealed to him.

00:09:42.960 --> 00:09:49.120
"You could play the scenes where you&nbsp;
switch from comedy to drama in a line."

00:09:49.760 --> 00:09:54.160
As an example, Dafoe singled out the scene&nbsp;
in which Osborn and Green Goblin have a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.160 --> 00:09:59.520
conversation with one another in a mirror. He&nbsp;
told GQ that director Sam Raimi even gave him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.520 --> 00:10:05.120
Robert Louis Stevenson's novel The Strange Case&nbsp;
of Dr. Jekyll and Mr. Hyde to read for the scene.

00:10:05.120 --> 00:10:09.200
During his sit-down with GQ, Dafoe&nbsp;
repeatedly mentions how much fun he had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.200 --> 00:10:12.480
during his experience on Spider-Man.&nbsp;
And it must have been worth it, too,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.480 --> 00:10:16.800
considering the role's considerable physical&nbsp;
challenges. In a behind-the-scenes feature on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.800 --> 00:10:20.480
the Spider-Man DVD, Dafoe claimed&nbsp;
to have endured between "40 and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.480 --> 00:10:25.680
50 hours of fittings" of the Goblin suit&nbsp;
before the design was finalized. Dafoe said:

00:10:25.680 --> 00:10:29.360
"It was really uncomfortable to wear.&nbsp;
It was so tight and so heavy that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.360 --> 00:10:32.960
after a whole day in that thing, you&nbsp;
felt like you had just gone 12 rounds."

00:10:33.680 --> 00:10:38.160
Early in June 2019, the anonymous&nbsp;
leaker known as "Roger Wardell"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.160 --> 00:10:41.920
unleashed a torrent of supposed&nbsp;
leaks about MCU's Phase 4.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.920 --> 00:10:46.400
While many of these leaks remain unconfirmed,&nbsp;
it's only fair to mention that this same person&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.400 --> 00:10:51.280
leaked accurate details about Avengers:&nbsp;
Endgame months before the movie's release.

00:10:51.280 --> 00:10:55.840
Among other things, Wardell had some major&nbsp;
claims about the Green Goblin. According to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.840 --> 00:11:00.000
the alleged leaks, Phase 4's primary&nbsp;
antagonist won't be a purple guy from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:02.880
outer space, but someone much closer to home. Yes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.880 --> 00:11:08.880
Wardell claims that Norman Osborn will&nbsp;
supposedly serve as the next Big Bad of the MCU.

00:11:08.880 --> 00:11:14.720
Of course, it's now known that Willem Dafoe's&nbsp;
version of Green Goblin is on his way to the MCU.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.720 --> 00:11:18.240
Dafoe will reprise the villainous&nbsp;
role in Spider-Man: No Way Home,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.240 --> 00:11:22.560
but it remains to be seen whether he'll&nbsp;
replace Thanos as Marvel's main villain.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.560 --> 00:11:26.960
Considering Osborn and the other Spider-Man&nbsp;
villains appearing will be travelers from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.960 --> 00:11:31.280
other parts of the multiverse, one of the more&nbsp;
likely outcomes of the story would seem to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.280 --> 00:11:35.920
their return to their respective realities.&nbsp;
Not only that, but Jonathan Majors' Kang the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.920 --> 00:11:41.040
Conqueror has been frequently cited as the&nbsp;
true Big Bad of Phase 4. But it's likely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.040 --> 00:11:45.600
that Kevin Feige and company won't be able to&nbsp;
resist introducing their own version of Norman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.600 --> 00:11:50.880
Osborn at some point or another — and who knows&nbsp;
what might happen with him? Only time will tell.

